#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166639 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "954f8004-35b4-58ec-be26-2beccfd5f8e4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Equation That Generates a Slope Field Using Equilibrium Solutions"}] :question/id #uuid "20b4b166-5dfe-5908-89b1-3f0a15afb59b" :question/math-academy-id "e-9306" :question/problem "Which of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3220/Source/Images/ebef86d8bf088f7bfceaaed94999c6cd.png)\n\n1. $y' = y^2(y-1)$\n2. $y' = y(y-1)(y-4)$\n3. $y' = -(y-1)^2(y-4)$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3220/Source/Images/fad2dd38b97e530e13888329b587e157.png)\n\nNotice that the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $y = 1$ and $y = 4$, as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each equation:\n\n- The equation $y' = y^2(y-1)$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $y=0$ and $y=1$.\n- The equation $y' = -y(y-1)(y-4)$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $y=1$ and $y=4$, but it also evaluates to zero when $y=0$, which is not behavior exhibited by our slope field.\n- The equation $y' =-(y-1)^2(y-4)$ gives the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $y=1$ and $y=4$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166640 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ca086fd-9732-5c4b-9cb0-168433d79203" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Equation That Generates a Slope Field Using the Sign of the Slopes"}] :question/id #uuid "9a55e013-edaa-5c6a-97af-cd42f94f6ed9" :question/math-academy-id "e-9307" :question/problem "The diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3220/Source/Images/86ab6710d8a01fe1648d5b39a316ed5b.png)\n\n1. $y = -y^2(y+4)$\n2. $y = y(y-4)^2$\n3. $y = y(y+4)$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3220/Source/Images/d4a4ac0f384c15b61fcaed3773ab2b76.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $y=-4$ and $y=0$. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should equal zero on both of these lines.Of the given equations, the only equations that satisfy this condition are the following:\n$y^{′}|=-y^{2}(y + 4); y^{′}|= y(y + 4)$\n- Second, the lines $y=-4$ and $y=0$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\nRegion$y < -4$$-4 < y < 0$$y > 0$Slope$\\nearrow$$\\searrow$$\\searrow$Sign$\\color{blue}+$$\\color{red}-$$\\color{red}-$\nOf the remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n$y' = -y^2(y+4)$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is\n\n$$\ny' = -y^2(y+4)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166641 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fba5f68e-15da-5804-b6d9-d72ce6acae59" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Equation That Generates a Slope Field by Identifying Asymptotic Behavior"}] :question/id #uuid "26be6eb9-9cd0-596d-aa0f-8912400a498f" :question/math-academy-id "e-9308" :question/problem "Which of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3220/Source/Images/0ef8e5a6169e508db61f76dfc5ec0e3a.png)\n\n1. $y' = \\dfrac{y}{y+1}$\n2. $y'= \\dfrac1{y^2+y}$\n3. $y' = \\dfrac{2}{y+1}$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3220/Source/Images/e919936fc92a4b4110ef32705d009b5c.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- The slopes of the slope field become vertical as $y\\to -1$.\n- The slopes of the slope field become horizontal as $y\\to\\pm\\infty$.\n- The slopes of the slope field are positive for $y > -1$ and negative for $y < -1$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- The equation $y' = \\dfrac{y}{y+1}$ does *not* give the required behavior. Notice that\n$\\dfrac{y}{y+1} = \\dfrac{1}{\\left(1+\\dfrac1y\\right)}\\to 1 \\quad\\text{as} \\quad y\\to \\pm\\infty$.\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for this differential equation do not become horizontal as $y\\to\\pm\\infty$.\n- The equation $y' = \\dfrac1{y^2+y}= \\dfrac1{y(y+1)}$ does *not* give the required behavior. The slopes of the slope field for this differential equation become vertical for *both* $y \\to -1$ *and* $y \\to 0$,\nwhich is behavior not exhibited by our slope field.\n- The equation $y' = \\dfrac{2}{y+1}$ gives the required behavior. Notice that $y'\\to 0$ as $y\\to\\pm\\infty$, and $y'\\to \\infty$ as $y\\to -1$. Also, the right-hand side of this equation is positive for $y > -1$ and negative for $y < -1$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166643 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "800080f9-db79-5218-acaf-04040ab28a5d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Equation That Generates a Slope Field Using the Sign of the Slopes and Factoring"}] :question/id #uuid "f128f444-1d32-5343-a729-963030131c76" :question/math-academy-id "e-9314" :question/problem "The diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3220/Source/Images/68e980c93a3dde61e9b3777f266b0be6.png)\n\n1. $y' = y^3 - 4y^2 + 4y$\n2. $y' = y^2 - 2y$\n3. $y' = y^3 + y^2$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3220/Source/Images/ba11bc7d92bbbefc467705ceeece8cea.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slope field is zero along the lines $y = 0$ and $y = 2$. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on these lines.Of the given equations, the only two that satisfy this condition are\n$y'= y^3 - 4y^2 + 4y = y(y^2 - 4y + 4) = y(y-2)^2$\nand\n$y'= y^2 - 2y = y(y-2)$.\n- Second, the lines $y = 0$ and $y = 2$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\nRegion$y < 0$$0 < y < 2$$y > 2$Slope$\\searrow$$\\nearrow$$\\nearrow$Sign$\\color{red}-$$\\color{blue}+$$\\color{blue}+$\nOf the two remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n$y ' = y^3 - 4y^2 + 4y =y(y-2)^2$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is\n\n$$\ny' =y^3 - 4y^2 + 4y\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167015 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ca086fd-9732-5c4b-9cb0-168433d79203" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Equation That Generates a Slope Field Using the Sign of the Slopes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c128a86e-d78e-5baa-b737-36cd9a6d2ae8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{′} =-\\frac{1}{2}y(y + 2)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163509} {:answer/id #uuid "26f0c3c5-092f-510c-8fa1-30a9171632de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{′} = \\frac{1}{2}y^{2}(y + 2)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163510} {:answer/id #uuid "369a5254-639f-5506-bdb5-79a54221cefd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{′} = \\frac{1}{2}y^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163511} {:answer/id #uuid "395d94d3-6382-5b46-8e79-8d780cb8ae06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{′} = \\frac{1}{2}y(y + 2)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163512} {:answer/id #uuid "7c8b04e7-6448-5bb0-90b4-b3b9b2908fff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{′} =-\\frac{1}{2}(y + 2)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163513}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c128a86e-d78e-5baa-b737-36cd9a6d2ae8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{′} =-\\frac{1}{2}y(y + 2)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163509} :answer-field/id #uuid "1fa0a6ee-3caa-5179-b82c-b58cb6511513" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163508}] :question/id #uuid "45ddced2-4265-581a-ad59-d353bca932c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-115466" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3220/Source/Images/q-115466.png)\n\nThe diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167017 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fba5f68e-15da-5804-b6d9-d72ce6acae59" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Equation That Generates a Slope Field by Identifying Asymptotic Behavior"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c2c6a81-3f03-541a-9325-21f5f0c5abf7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163521} {:answer/id #uuid "dc2a884b-11ee-57b3-9e7c-6ad6ec23142f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163522} {:answer/id #uuid "e07814c4-1d3f-5ce3-ad92-37639e80d539" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163523} {:answer/id #uuid "302b2a05-5076-5cad-8abe-249eefc23414" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163524} {:answer/id #uuid "4334b047-6d7a-580d-951e-2708af4e7512" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163525}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dc2a884b-11ee-57b3-9e7c-6ad6ec23142f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163522} :answer-field/id #uuid "1083c99a-8c83-5801-97ba-64a13cca592d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163520}] :question/id #uuid "85b66c06-8b49-5d46-861f-2424f521305f" :question/math-academy-id "q-115479" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3220/Source/Images/q-115479.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n1. $y^{′} = \\frac{y}{y - 1}$\n2. $y^{′} = \\frac{1}{y}$\n3. $y^{′} = \\frac{1}{y^{2}}$"}]}
